17592186045417] [17592186188449 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186189089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188449 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188449 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4886bded-bc52-550f-be94-ef03345f5d14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188511 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188511 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188511 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188511 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188511 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188511 #edb/ref 1045 "Conditional Variance for Discrete Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188511 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186189169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188511 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188511 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "2c1b60fe-19a1-5c76-8d58-a10c1ae4790c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189061 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189061 #edb/ref 1061 2211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189061 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1bec56da-aad1-51b3-af70-e54f081bf7c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189061 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189062 #edb/ref 1061 2212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189062 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b2ee3a4d-b10f-5633-a0b0-9087e7785ae4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189062 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189089 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189089 #edb/ref 1061 37856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189089 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "43d1c988-b5bd-5711-8f19-90e40ffc0bcf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189089 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189090 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189090 #edb/ref 1061 37857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189090 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "96db70eb-88bd-53e8-beda-04d15d1f59e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189090 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189167 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189167 #edb/ref 1061 38837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189167 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2ef086e9-8163-573e-9575-a78051c9420a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189167 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189168 #edb/ref 1061 38849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189168 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ac37dad4-4109-5b6c-a2d0-f6c46e058597" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189168 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189169 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189169 #edb/ref 1061 38850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189169 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "12bb1f79-4af3-56c1-b495-e654c2cf5d1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189169 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189183 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189183 #edb/ref 1061 38984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189183 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7f447a96-6d58-5356-934d-4d4a94738359" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189183 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189360 #edb/ref 1064 "To find the first derivative of a parametric curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}.\n$$\n\nA formula for the second derivative of a parametric curve can be found by applying the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{dy}{dx}) \\\\  & =\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})\\cdot \\frac{d\\theta }{dx} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see an example of how to apply this." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189360 #edb/ref 1065 1124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189360 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "16aecece-e4cd-56e7-bdd9-a19c15eb3136" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189360 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating Second Derivatives of Polar Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189368 #edb/ref 1064 "It's often helpful to use the double-angle formula for sine when computing the slope of the tangent to a polar curve, as this often makes the differentiation easier.\n\nLet's consider the polar curve $r = 1-2\\cos\\theta,$ whose graph is shown below \n\n![](images/53/53c660d25a8bec2346cacd95d70fb3f4ee27afe039bd23cbe7affa3cf04eb09a.png)\n\nRecall that the slope of the tangent (in terms of $\\theta$) is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r = 1-2\\cos\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2{cos}^{2}\\theta , \\\\ y & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the double-angle formula $\\color{black}\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$ to simplify our expression for $y.$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2{cos}^{2}\\theta )^{'} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{'}-(2{cos}^{2}\\theta )^{'} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )-(2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}2\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}(\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta )}^{'} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{'}-{(\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta )}^{'} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the slope of the tangent is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{'}(\\theta )}{{x}^{'}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2\\text{ }\\,\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta }{2\\text{ }\\,\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189368 #edb/ref 1065 6236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189368 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "adced1fe-4c40-50d1-a9b4-12fcf213b6e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189368 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189401 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables, where the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is given by $\\mu_{X \\mid y}$. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\begin{bmatrix}X\\, & \\,Y=y\\end{bmatrix} = \\mu_{X \\mid y}\n$$\n\nThe **conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$** is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}\\begin{bmatrix}X\\, & \\,Y=y\\end{bmatrix} = \\textrm{E}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}(X - \\mu_{X \\mid y} \\big)^2 \\, & \\, Y=y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis is similar to the definition of $\\textrm{Var}[X]$ in that it represents the average (squared) distance of the random variable $X$ from the mean. The difference here is that the expected values are now dependent on the outcome of $Y$.\n\nSimilarly to the case of $\\textrm{Var}[X]$, this often isn't the easiest formula to use in practice. However, it can be shown that the definition of conditional variance is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}X^{2} & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{X\\begin{vmatrix}y}^{2} \\\\ = [∑_(x)x^{2}f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid y)] - μ_{X\\end{vmatrix}y}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\, \\mid \\, y)$ is the conditional PMF of $X$ given $Y$. Note that these formulas are analogous to those for $\\textrm{Var}[X]$.\n\nFinally, we have an analogous definition for the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = x\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}(Y - μ_{Y \\mid x})^{2} & X = x\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\\begin{bmatrix}Y^{2} & X = x\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{Y\\begin{vmatrix}x}^{2} \\\\ = [∑_(y)y^{2}f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x)] - μ_{Y\\end{vmatrix}x}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189401 #edb/ref 1065 6431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189401 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ce4fb1c3-94ee-51c3-b10d-ce6ea0c7ff38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189401 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192754 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192754 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192754 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192754 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192754 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192754 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192754 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192754 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192754 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192754 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a Probability Over an Unbounded Interv